`mode` is simply an `int` which you can pass into the method.
I also don't see any UI-related stuff in this method, it's just that the method is in the `fragment` class. But you can create a `manager` class which only contains logic. The method would looks something like this
```java 
public boolean aa(File file, int mode, boolean parent) {
    if (file.isDirectory() && file.isHidden()) {
        return true;
    }
    if (parent && !file.isDirectory() && !filterFileExtension.isEmpty()
            && (mode == MODE_FILE || mode == MODE_FILE_AND_DIR)) {
        final String ext = getExtension(file);
        return filterFileExtension.equalsIgnoreCase(ext);
    }
    return parent;
}
```
You would then also move your `getExtension()` and `filterFileExtension` into that class. Or maybe pass in `filterFileExtension` as parameter to the method. You could pass in `parent` as an lambda which you can then lazily evaluate if it is needed (kotlin it's possible, java maybe?)

and called from the fragment like 
```java
return new Manager().aa(file, mode, super.isItemVisible(file));
```

----

On a side note. I checked out your code and Android Studio says that the `ext != null` is unnecessary because `equalsIgnoreCase` already performs a null check. So please remove that.
